export const LESSONS={
timing:{title:'Make the clock visible.',objective:'CH8-01 · Separate signal information, execution and the earned holding interval.',body:`<p>A backtest reconstructs a rule’s hypothetical actions from supplied data. Before calculation, freeze this rule: after close t, average the latest three closes including t. If close t is strictly above that mean, target weight 1; otherwise target 0. Execute at open t+1 and earn the open t+1→open t+2 return. Do not tune the three-close window.</p><div class="formula">Signal after close t → execute open t+1 → mark open t+2</div><p>Worked: Jan 2–4 closes are 100,101,102, mean101. Jan4 close102>101 implies target1. It first buys at Jan5 open103, then marks at Jan6 open100. Its first gross holding return is 100/103−1=−2.9126%. Using the earlier Jan4 close as the execution price would pretend to trade at a price already used to calculate the decision.</p><p>Warm-up rows provide three closes and earn no strategy return here. The final close is not used to make an unexecutable signal. Each holding interval ends at the next open; final liquidation is separately charged at the last eligible open. Prices are original positive synthetic values, not split-adjusted market data. They have no distributions or corporate actions to adjust.</p>`,reflection:'Draw signal close → next open execution → following open mark. Explain why each date must be explicit.',criteria:'I used only known signal information and kept the next-open execution lag.'},
positions:{title:'Translate the rule without hindsight.',objective:'CH8-02 · Reconstruct target weights and detect future-data contamination.',body:`<p>A target weight of 1 means fully invested; 0 means cash. This example permits no leverage or short positions. Cash earns zero, so a cash interval has zero gross return before any transaction costs. The strict “above” condition sends a tie to cash.</p><p>Worked second signal: Jan3–5 closes101,102,101 have mean101.3333. Jan5 close101 is below that mean: target0 at Jan6 open100. The prior position1 was held Jan5→Jan6. At Jan6 open it sells to cash and incurs exit costs; it does not retroactively avoid the loss it already earned.</p><p>Predict: if the final future close changes, can the first position change? It must not. Each target uses only the three closes ending on its own signal date. A future perturbation test detects many timing mistakes, although passing it does not prove the entire implementation correct. Our tests also check hand arithmetic, matched benchmarks and costs.</p><p>All supplied runs, including disappointing ones, belong in a trial log. Viewing the transfer data in this prototype counts as exposure. A label “evaluation” alone is not proof of independent validation. Chapter9 prepares an honest plan and disclosure; Chapter10 reports evidence within those limits.</p>`,reflection:'Explain a transition from invested to cash and why changing future data must not rewrite past target weights.',criteria:'I preserve past positions under future perturbations and disclose all data exposure.'},
costs:{title:'Pay for entry and exit.',objective:'CH8-03 · Apply turnover costs before the period return and include final liquidation.',body:`<p>One basis point (bp) is 0.01%; 10 bps is .10%=.001 as a decimal. For target w and previous target w<sub>prev</sub>, turnover is |w−w<sub>prev</sub>|. The fee is cost rate c times this fraction of current wealth. Charge the fee first, then earn the holding return.</p><div class="formula">V after interval = V before × (1−c|w−w<sub>prev</sub>|) × (1+w r)<br>Final liquidation = V × (1−c w<sub>last</sub>)</div><p>Worked: £1000, previous0,target1,10bps,+10% return →1000×.999×1.1=£1098.90. Final exit costs another £1.0989, leaving £1097.8011. A 1→0 transition also has turnover1. No change in a 0/1 target means zero turnover. This simplified discrete all-in/cash rule avoids intermediate-weight drift.</p><p>For the supplied Jan example the first step is £1000×.999×100/103=£969.9029. The following exit at10bps reduces that cash balance too. If prices are flat but the benchmark buys and later sells, its final wealth is £1000×.999²=£998.001. A cost-free result is not a net result.</p><p>No additional bid–ask spread, market impact, slippage, taxes, default, borrow fees or cash interest is modelled. This fee is a teaching proxy, not a complete execution model. Increasing a nonnegative fee with an unchanged fixed position path cannot improve ending wealth here. Do not search fees or windows for the nicest return.</p>`,reflection:'Show bps→decimal conversion, initial entry and final exit arithmetic. Name omitted trading frictions.',criteria:'I included every 0/1 transition and final liquidation, and applied costs before returns.'},
benchmark:{title:'Match the comparison window.',objective:'CH8-04 · Evaluate the rule and buy-and-hold over identical eligible opens with consistent costs.',body:`<p>The benchmark starts with the same £1000, buys at the strategy’s first eligible execution open, holds through the same final eligible open and sells there. It pays the same per-notional fee on entry and final exit. Both series omit warm-up performance and use zero-interest cash, no leverage and the same prices.</p><p>On the development table, both eligible windows are Jan5 open£103→Jan17 open£108. Cost-free benchmark growth is108/103−1=4.8544%. At10bps its final wealth is1000×.999×108/103×.999=£1046.4477. The strategy uses different weights inside these dates, so it can have different growth and risk. Ending wealth alone is not a complete risk comparison.</p><p>Predict: would a benchmark starting at Jan2 open£100 be comparable? No: it includes days excluded from the strategy. A strategy can appear better or worse simply because dates differ. Use the table’s explicit signal, entry and exit dates to inspect alignment.</p><p>Development dates are Jan2–17,2025. A separate, already-visible transfer table spans Feb3–12,2025, with eligible opens Feb6–12. These tiny authored segments are teaching cases, not market evidence or a secure holdout. This chapter does not establish profitability, robustness, out-of-sample validation or an investable recommendation.</p>`,reflection:'Describe the same-date benchmark and distinguish a teaching transfer case from independent market validation.',criteria:'I matched eligible opens, wealth, cost convention and assumptions, and avoided a profitability claim.'}
};
export const SOURCES=[['Bailey et al.: repeated trials and the probability of backtest overfitting','https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf'],['Investor.gov: fees and expenses affect investment outcomes','https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/mutual-funds-and-exchange-traded-1']];
